{"question_id":"passuming-the-earth-to-be-a-sphere-of-uniform-mass-density-jee-main-physics-motion-w5zyneo3x1sntrhg","qKey":"passuming-the-earth-to-be-a-sphere-of-uniform-mass-density-jee-main-physics-motion-w5zyneo3x1sntrhg","permalink":"passuming-the-earth-to-be-a-sphere-of-uniform-mass-density-jee-main-physics-motion-w5zyneo3x1sntrhg","paperTitle":"JEE Main 2023 (Online) 10th April Morning Shift","year":2023,"subject":"physics","chapter":"gravitation","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"content":"<p>Assuming the earth to be a sphere of uniform mass density, the weight of a body at a depth <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.781ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.41ex\" height=\"2.765ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -877 2833.2 1222\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(800.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(1856.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311.6,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><rect width=\"736.7\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> from the surface of earth, if its weight on the surface of earth is 200 N, will be:</p>\n<p>(Given R = radius of earth)</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"100 N"},{"identifier":"B","content":"400 N"},{"identifier":"C","content":"300 N"},{"identifier":"D","content":"500 N"}],"correct_options":["A"],"explanation":"<p>The gravitational field strength, or equivalently, the weight of an object, decreases linearly from its surface value to zero at the center of a sphere of uniform mass density. This is because only the mass inside the radius at which the object is located contributes to the gravitational force at that location. </p>\n<p>If ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.781ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.41ex\" height=\"2.765ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -877 2833.2 1222\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(800.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(1856.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311.6,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><rect width=\"736.7\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) is the depth below the surface of the Earth, then the radius of the sphere contributing to the gravitational force at that depth is ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.781ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20.603ex\" height=\"2.765ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -877 9106.4 1222\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(981.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1981.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2782.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3838,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4819.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(5819.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311.6,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><rect width=\"736.7\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7073.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(8129.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311.6,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><rect width=\"736.7\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ).</p>\n<p>Since the gravitational force (or weight) decreases linearly with the radius in a sphere of uniform density, the weight of the object at depth ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.781ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.41ex\" height=\"2.765ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -877 2833.2 1222\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-widt